# ==========================================================================
#  Q115  A copy with no blank nodes left in it
# ==========================================================================
#
#  ASKS
#    Build a graph that says the same thing about disjointness, with every
#    blank node replaced by something nameable.
#
#  HOW IT WORKS
#    CONSTRUCT with a skolem IRI in the subject position. The list
#    structure disappears: instead of a chain of cells, each axiom gets a
#    flat set of bs:disjointMember triples. The result loads into any
#    engine, survives a round trip through a file, and can be diffed.
#
#  DIAGRAM
#        before -- 23 blank nodes, 46 triples of plumbing:
#
#          _:a --owl:members--> _:c1 --rdf:rest--> _:c2 --rdf:rest--> ...
#                                 |                  |
#                            rdf:first          rdf:first
#                                 v                  v
#                           bs:Settlement    bs:CouncilArea
#
#        after -- no blank nodes, no plumbing:
#
#          bt:axiom-bookshop-11    bs:disjointMember  bs:Bookshop ;
#                                  bs:disjointMember  bs:Person ;
#                                  ...
#          bt:axiom-councilarea-4  bs:disjointMember  bs:Settlement ;
#                                  ...
#
#        15 triples out, one per member, and every subject has a name.
#
#        What was lost: the order of the list, and the fact that OWL
#        reads it as one axiom rather than a bag of memberships. That is
#        the trade -- flattening is easier to query and no longer means
#        quite the same thing. Do it for reporting and for diffing, not
#        as a replacement for the original.
#
#        Sending the result somewhere: press Get All in the editor after
#        running a CONSTRUCT and the constructed triples come back as
#        Turtle you can save.
#
#
#  WHAT TO TAKE AWAY
#    - CONSTRUCT plus a skolem IRI is how you hand a blank-node structure
#      to something that cannot cope with blank nodes.
#    - Flattening a collection loses its order and its meaning as a
#      single axiom. Know what you are giving up.
#    - A graph with no blank nodes can be diffed, merged and quoted. That
#      is worth a lot when the data is under review.
#
#  DATA     bookshop-trail-1.1.ttl
#  LOAD IT  https://semantechs.co.uk/turtle-editor-viewer/?dot=https%3A%2F%2Fraw.githubusercontent.com%2Fpwin%2FSPARQL_Course%2Fmain%2Fdata%2Fbookshop-trail-1.1.ttl
#  RUNS ON  editor, holos, fuseki
#  SPEC     SPARQL 1.2 Query §16.2.1 Templates with Blank Nodes
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#x16-2-1-templates-with-blank-nodes
#           SPARQL 1.2 Query §2.4 Blank Node Identifiers in Query Results
#           https://www.w3.org/TR/sparql12-query/#x2-4-blank-node-identifiers-in-query-results
#  RETURNS  15 rows, on every engine that runs it
# ==========================================================================

PREFIX bt:  <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/>
PREFIX bs:  <https://example.org/bookshop-trail/schema#>
PREFIX rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>
PREFIX owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>

CONSTRUCT {
  ?id bs:disjointMember ?member .
}
WHERE {
  {
    SELECT ?axiom (COUNT(?m) AS ?size) (MIN(?local) AS ?firstMember)
    WHERE {
      ?axiom a           owl:AllDisjointClasses ;
             owl:members/rdf:rest*/rdf:first ?m .
      BIND( REPLACE(STR(?m), "^.*[/#]", "") AS ?local )
    }
    GROUP BY ?axiom
  }
  ?axiom owl:members/rdf:rest*/rdf:first ?member .
  BIND( IRI(CONCAT("https://example.org/bookshop-trail/axiom-",
                   LCASE(?firstMember), "-", STR(?size))) AS ?id )
}
